    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In 2017 and 2018, the federal government in Brazil enacted two substantial standards-based reforms, prompting states to create curricula based on the federal educational standards. This exploratory paper uses a novel computational sampling method, targeted sampling, to examine depictions of equity in postsecondary preparation in the newly published high school curricula from 17 states in Brazil and its Federal District. I discuss the findings in line with the literature on equitable access to post-secondary opportunities. I find that states’ high school curricula in Brazil rely heavily on legal documents and federal standards, providing few original discussions on pedagogical guidelines and support systems for equitable post-secondary preparation. Contrary to prevalent governmental discourse, I conclude that the reforms are unlikely to be drivers of equity. Future policy considerations are discussed.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Castro, F. (2022). Curricular limbo: content analysis of equitable support systems in Brazilian high school curricula. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/bx024yy6651",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
